{{short description|The legal and social status of prostitution in Balisca}}
{{Life in Balisca}} 
'''[[W:Prostitution|Prostitution]] in [[Balisca]]''' is legally and socially distinct from many other countries, operating under a legal framework that prioritizes individual freedom and worker protection over prohibition. The sale of sex between consenting adults is decriminalized, and the industry is heavily regulated, with a significant portion of the sector operating under a [[W:worker cooperative|worker-controlled cooperative model]]. This system is designed to protect the rights of [[W:Sex worker|sex workers]], ensure health and safety standards, and aggressively combat exploitation and [[W:Human trafficking|human trafficking]]. As a result, activities such as [[W:Pimping|pimping]], [[W:Forced prostitution|forced prostitution]], human trafficking, and any third-party enterprises that profit from the exploitation of sex workers are severely criminalized.

The sale of sex was first explicitly legalized in the city of [[Castejón]] in 1888, a landmark decision that recognized a longstanding culture of permissiveness regarding the practice in the [[District of Castejón|confederal capital]] since the 17th century. This legislation, while decriminalizing sex work, did not address the third parties who profited from it, such as pimps and traffickers. Confederal law, adopted in 1948, empowered sex workers to form worker cooperatives, allowing them to collectively manage their affairs, handle logistics, and ensure their safety.

The industry is subject to strict health and safety regulations, and sex workers are guaranteed access to periodic health checks and [[W:Sexual health|sexual health]] services (including testing, [[W:Contraceptives|contraceptives]], [[W:Pre-exposure prophylaxis for HIV prevention|PreP]], etc.) through Balisca's universal healthcare system.

